nsumes its notification, updates committed configuration and gathers committed inflight logs. processLogs sends command/barrier tuples to the asynchronous FSM worker; its lastApplied update means dispatch progress, whereas applySingle completes the future after FSM.Apply returns. A2 authority: follower timeout checks local voter eligibility; a candidate uses a campaign-local reply channel, electSelf increments and persists term through setCurrentTerm and requests one vote per configured voter, and requestVote checks membership, known leader, term, prior vote and log freshness before persisting a grant. A new leader allocates a fresh commitment object and dispatches a no-op. On loss of leadership it closes replication stop channels and fails pending log and verification futures; replication objects retain their own commitment references, so the observed accounting path is not an unconditional write into a subsequent leader's fresh commitment. Configuration admission waits for the previous configuration and a current-term entry to be committed, then changes the accounting voter set. Followers reject older AppendEntries terms and mismatched predecessor terms, replace conflicting suffixes, roll latest configuration back when truncation crosses it, and dispatch up to the received commit bound. The FSM interface independently states that Apply is called after majority commitment. These are observed mechanisms and interface requirements, not proof of their composition.\\\",\n  'remaining':[\n   'Core readiness remains incomplete: read replication-state construction/removal, leader lease/verification, snapshot installation and startup recovery, configuration validation, persistence setters and remaining FSM/client completion paths before recording a usable core_overview.',\n   'surface:Raft.appendEntries: commit advancement uses min(LeaderCommitIndex, local last index), while setNewLogs limits requests to MaxAppendEntries. Investigate whether a legal catch-up history can re